{% extends "layout.njk" %}

{% block article %}
<h2 id="startr-swap">Startr Swap</h2>
<p>Startr Swap swaps links and forms in place, with no build step. Drop it in, mark a region, and
  same-origin links stop reloading the page: they swap in place instead, wrapped in a CSS View
  Transition.</p>
<p>It fetches the page and parses it with <code>fetch</code> + <code>DOMParser</code>. There is no
  hidden iframe. An iframe performs a REAL navigation, so scripts in the response execute inside
  it, in the wrong document, silently, and every swap re-requests the page's stylesheets.
  <code>fetch</code> + <code>DOMParser</code> parses inert, and reports a redirect honestly.</p>
<p>Lineage is htmz (MIT, &copy; Lean Rada), whose hash-names-the-target convention it keeps.</p>

<h2 id="install">Install</h2>

{% highlight "html" %}
<script defer src="https://startr.style/v1/swap.js"
        integrity="sha384-8Y08Zw/VDvaVCy/KtaWr74UVCwN9nFkiMksvlmcQyQVecrpdmPkOH84i4aaIN6DX"
        crossorigin="anonymous"></script>
<main data-swap> ... </main>
{% endhighlight %}

<p><code>/v1/swap.js</code> is immutable and cached for a year. <code>/swap.js</code> is the rolling
  build this site uses itself and carries no integrity guarantee. The sha256 is published at
  <code>/v1/swap.js.sha256</code>.</p>

<h2 id="markup">Markup</h2>
<p>The API is three attributes and four events, and that is the whole thing.</p>
<table>
  <thead>
    <tr>
      <th>Attribute</th>
      <th>Placed on</th>
      <th>Meaning</th>
    </tr>
  </thead>
  <tbody>
    <tr>
      <td><code>data-swap</code></td>
      <td>a region</td>
      <td>Marks it swappable. A value narrows which links are taken over to a path prefix, and
        nested regions inherit it.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>data-swap-target</code></td>
      <td>a control</td>
      <td>Updates THAT region, by selector, instead of the one this control lives in.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr>
      <td><code>data-swap-off</code></td>
      <td>a control</td>
      <td>Never swaps this one.</td>
    </tr>
  </tbody>
</table>

<p>Only the region swaps; everything outside it stays. A page whose chrome differs outside the
  region should not declare one, and links into it should carry <code>data-swap-off</code>. This
  site does exactly that for its home page, whose hero sits outside <code>&lt;main&gt;</code>.</p>

<h2 id="events">Events</h2>
<p>All four fire on the region, bubbling, cancelable.</p>
<ul>
  <li><code>swap:before</code> — cancel to abandon the swap; the browser navigates normally.</li>
  <li><code>swap:navigate</code> — cancel to own the address bar yourself.</li>
  <li><code>swap:after</code> — content is in.</li>
  <li><code>swap:error</code> — cancel to suppress the fallback navigation.</li>
</ul>
<p>Regions nest, so sub-swaps cost no syntax at all. A pager inside a region updates that region
  and leaves the page alone, declaring nothing. A sub-swap region does need an <code>id</code>,
  because the response has to be able to name the piece coming back.</p>

<h2 id="things-every-consumer-hits">Things every consumer hits</h2>
<ol>
  <li><strong>There is no <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> default, deliberately.</strong> The first
    version claimed <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> when no region was declared. Any document can end up
    running this file — a host application that adopts a fetched page's scripts adopts this one
    too — and a <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> default would then take over every link in a document
    that never asked for it. <strong>A region must be declared.</strong></li>
  <li><strong>The click listener CAPTURES; the submit listener does not.</strong> A single-page
    router binds its own document-level click listener at boot, so in the bubble phase it always
    wins. Capture puts a declared region ahead of a generic router. Submit stays in the bubble
    phase on purpose, because attribute-driven libraries bind submits on the FORM, and capturing
    would take their submits away from them.</li>
  <li><strong>A POST that redirected must push history; a POST that answered in place must
    not.</strong> Post/Redirect/Get lands on a plain address a reader can reload and share. A POST
    that answered in place has an address that only accepts POST, so pushing it hands the reader a
    reload that 405s. <code>res.redirected</code> is the exact signal.</li>
  <li><strong>An inline <code>&lt;script&gt;</code> in a swapped region never runs.</strong> The
    library re-runs only <code>script[src]</code>. Re-create inline scripts from the host, as this
    site does in <code>src/_includes/swap-host.njk</code> with a MutationObserver.</li>
  <li><strong>Under a View Transition, <code>swap:after</code> fires before the update callback has
    run</strong>, so it may see the old content. Observe the region instead. Fix planned for
    v1.1.</li>
</ol>

<h2 id="size">Size</h2>
<p>13,802 bytes raw, 5,343 gzipped (as the gate measures it). htmx is 50,917 raw and 16,367 gzipped. The gate
  <code>scripts/gates/startr-swap/check.py</code> holds a ceiling of 8,192 gzipped.</p>

<h2 id="demo">Demo</h2>
<p><a href="/demo/swap/a.html">/demo/swap/a.html</a> and <code>b.html</code> swap between each
  other.</p>
<p><a href="/demo/swap/none.html">/demo/swap/none.html</a> loads the script, declares no region,
  keeps a <code>&lt;main&gt;</code> on purpose, and must navigate normally.</p>

